        "## 22.24 Common mistakes\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Confusing logits and probabilities.**  Softmax converts logits into probabilities.  Cross-entropy should usually be computed using a numerically stable log-softmax.\n",
        "2. **Averaging over padding tokens.**  Padding tokens should be masked out of token-level losses.\n",
        "3. **Using a learning rate without checking gradient scale.**  The product $\\eta\\|g\\|$ controls step size.\n",
        "4. **Assuming smaller training loss means better model.**  Training loss and test performance can move differently.\n",
        "5. **Treating Adam as magic.**  Adam rescales coordinates using gradient moments; it still requires learning-rate tuning and regularization.\n",
        "6. **Ignoring random variation.**  Mini-batch order, initialization, and hardware can change training trajectories.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 22.25 Worked calculation\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a three-class model outputs probabilities\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p=(0.20,0.60,0.20)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the correct class is class $2$.  With one-hot target\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e_2=(0,1,0),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the gradient with respect to logits is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\nabla_z\\ell=p-e_2=(0.20,-0.40,0.20).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If the logit model is $z=Wh+b$ with\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h=(1,2)^T,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\nabla_W\\ell=(p-e_2)h^T\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{pmatrix}\n",
        "0.20 \\\\\n",
        "-0.40 \\\\\n",
        "0.20\n",
        "\\end{pmatrix}\n",
        "\\begin{pmatrix}\n",
        "1 & 2\n",
        "\\end{pmatrix}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{pmatrix}\n",
        "0.20 & 0.40 \\\\\n",
        "-0.40 & -0.80 \\\\\n",
        "0.20 & 0.40\n",
        "\\end{pmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus gradient descent subtracts a positive multiple of this matrix.  The row for the correct class increases in the direction of $h$, while the rows for incorrect classes decrease in that direction.\n",
